## ===========================================================================
## THE WANDERING MERCENARIES — the `hunted` state's pressure model
##
## The sweep (change document, "The sweep", "The sweep's postures"): once Jack
## has heard the voices in the Alley and the story is `hunted`, a pair of
## mercenaries works the market looking for her. They are NOT a chase — the
## chase begins on Teisha's TE20 trade — they are the pressure that makes
## standing still dangerous. Converted from `story.ni:2037-2182`.
##
## The source's machine, in turns, during its `Avoiding Soldiers` scene:
##
##   1. ARRIVAL. Jack waits in a room; once she has waited longer than a rolled
##      1-3 turn timeout, the mercenaries arrive in her room, `oblivious`.
##   2. DWELL. Oblivious mercenaries in her room for two turns warn her; for
##      three, they spot her and become `approaching`.
##   3. CAPTURE. Approaching mercenaries grab her when a countdown runs out;
##      two turns after that the captain arrives and the game ends in death.
##   4. ESCAPE. Moving while they are `approaching` loses them. Moving while
##      they hold her is refused; attacking them breaks the grip and drops
##      them back to `approaching`, and then she can run.
##   5. TEXTURE. Idle oblivious mercenaries have a 1-in-5 chance per turn of
##      an atmosphere line.
##
## How the beats map onto the timers below (ADR-325, "What the mercenaries
## become"):
##
##   * The re-rolled 1-3 `timeout` is the player's `waiting` — two named turns,
##      interrupted one chance in two, restarted whenever she moves while
##      hunted. It expires wherever she is standing, and that is where they
##      arrive: `move it here`.
##   * "In the location for two turns" is `search` in its `lingering` turn,
##      carrying the warning line; "three turns" is `search` expiring.
##   * The countdown of 1 between "There he is!" and "Gotcha!" is `lunge`, a
##      timer with no named turns.
##   * "The leader arrives in two turns from now" (`story.ni:2120`) is
##      `capture`: one named turn, then the captain.
##   * The countdown of 2 after a break-free is `recovery`; when it runs out
##      they lunge again.
##
## What changed in the port, and why:
##
##   * The third posture is `aggressive`, not the source's `grabbing` (David,
##     2026-08-23): it is a standing state the rest of the model reads, and a
##     standing state wants a posture word. There is no fourth posture for the
##     break-free window: the mercenaries are `approaching` while `recovery`
##     runs, which is what lets her dash away during it.
##   * Losing them: the source takes them off-stage; so does this (`move it
##     offstage`). Any move she makes while they are not holding her sends
##     them offstage and puts every clock back, so a search never finishes in
##     a room she has already left. The dash-away line is only for the move
##     that shakes off an approach. The clause is guarded `while the
##     wandering mercenaries is not here`: it runs AFTER a room's own
##     `after the player entering` on the same move, so a move that ends
##     with the pair already in her new room is an arrival beat (the tumble
##     from the roof, `eavesdrop.chord`) and must not be undone.
##   * The source suppresses the warning and the spotting when "the
##     mercenaries will not recognise us" (woolen cap off, captain not yet
##     wise — `story.ni:2093-2099`). That branch is DROPPED, not deferred
##     (change document, "The recognition rule, sharpened", 2026-08-23):
##     anything except the dress and new hat leaves Jack exposed, so no
##     suppression is ever keyed to the woolen cap, and the "looks right at
##     you … don't recognize you" arrival variant is not carried in its
##     2009 form. The suppression state arrives with the escape sequence
##     (dress + hat), nowhere before it.
##   * `kick` is the story's own dispatch action (below): the `kick-escape`
##     trait forces the source's shin line where a plain attack rolls the
##     narrow-escape pool.
##
## On the player, in `secret-letter.story`: `on going` refuses while they hold
## her, and `after going while hunted` restarts `waiting`. The sweep opens on
## the climb down from the roof after the briefing (`eavesdrop.chord`, the
## Alley's `after the player entering while hunted, once`): the pair rushes
## into the alley `approaching` with `lunge` running, and Jack's first move
## out shakes them off and starts `waiting` — the source's own opening
## (`story.ni:1725`).
##
## THE SWEEP READS HER ONLY WHERE THEY STAND (2026-08-29): `search`, `lunge`
## and `recovery` step forward only while the pair is in Jack's room. Under
## ADR-328 D3 an expiry clause fires wherever the player is, and an
## AUTHORIAL move — the slide's `move the player`, the let-go drop — never
## fires `when the player moves`, so without the guard a search begun at the
## Top of the Post ran on to `aggressive` while she was on the wire and
## held her behind the fruit stall with nobody there. A search she has
## walked away from now simply lapses; the next arrival is `waiting`'s.
##
## The stallkeeper talk-refusals in the mercenaries' presence
## (`story.ni:1875-1879`) live below as the `wary` trait, composed on the ten
## keepers. Not here, on purpose: the conspicuous shopper (`story.ni:3395-3420`)
## and the noisy theft rule — each is its own increment against this model.
##
## The attack reaction is ONE `on attacking` clause, not on+after: an `on`
## clause's first phrase becomes the action's message, where an `after`
## reaction can only append — which is what kept the stdlib "Your attack has
## no effect" line printing ahead of the break-free text. Same mutations,
## phrases, and postures as the block ADR-325 records.
##
## Every line below is Gentry's, carried from `story.ni:2037-2182`.
## ===========================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CLOCKS
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define timer waiting for the player
  pausing
  loitering
  interrupted one chance in 2
end timer

define timer search for the wandering mercenaries
  arriving
  lingering
    Those mercenaries are getting uncomfortably close. You'd better get going
    before they notice you!
  meanwhile, one chance in 5
    phrase merc-idle
end timer

define timer lunge for the wandering mercenaries
end timer

define timer capture for the wandering mercenaries
  held
end timer

define timer recovery for the wandering mercenaries
  reeling
end timer

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE MERCENARIES
##
## Off-stage until the first arrival.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the wandering mercenaries
  a person, plural
  kick-escape
  aka mercenaries, mercenary, thugs, thug, soldiers, soldier, bravos, men, pair
  pronouns they
  states, reversible: oblivious, approaching, aggressive
  carries the sword

  Rough and ugly, with dirty clothes and mean, scarred faces. They carry
  serious-looking swords on their hips.

  when the player's waiting expires
    move the wandering mercenaries here
    change the wandering mercenaries to oblivious
    restart search
    phrase merc-arrival
  end when

  when search expires, while the wandering mercenaries is here
    change the wandering mercenaries to approaching
    start lunge
    phrase merc-spotted
  end when

  when lunge expires, while the wandering mercenaries is here
    change the wandering mercenaries to aggressive
    start capture
    phrase merc-grab
  end when

  when capture expires
    kill the player
      The captain soon arrives, followed by two more of his men. One of them
      grabs your other arm as their leader smiles. 'Good work, dims. Got
      ourselves a nice little commission with this one.' And before you can
      react, he takes a sack of black cloth from his belt and jerks it down
      over your head.

      Everything goes dark. You can't breathe. You struggle, but you can no
      more break the iron grip on your arms than you could uproot a tree. The
      men holding you curse. Dimly, you hear shouts from the crowd — someone
      protesting this rough treatment of a child.

      'Don't worry, m'lady,' laughs the mercenary leader. 'We'll treat the
      little rat nice an' gentle. Like this, see?'

      Something huge and heavy crashes into your head, and the rest of your
      senses are yanked away. Why they wanted you, you never awake to find out.
  end when

  when recovery expires, while the wandering mercenaries is here
    start lunge
  end when

  when the player's exposure expires, while the player is in the Fruit Stall and Jack is dressed
    change Jack to identified
    move the wandering mercenaries here
    change the wandering mercenaries to approaching
    start lunge
    phrase boots-spotted
  end when

  when the player's tent-escape expires, while the player is in Inside the Silk Tent
    move the wandering mercenaries here
    change the wandering mercenaries to aggressive
    phrase tent-caught
    kill the player mercenary-brutality
  end when

  when the player's tent-escape expires, while the player is in the Base of the Center Post
    phrase pole-escape-begins
    start the player's pole-escape
  end when

  when the player's tent-escape expires, while the player is in the Top of the Post
    phrase pole-escape-begins
    move the wandering mercenaries to the Base of the Center Post
    phrase pole-safe
    start the player's pole-problem
  end when

  when the player's pole-escape expires, while the player is not in the Top of the Post
    move the wandering mercenaries here
    change the wandering mercenaries to aggressive
    phrase pole-caught
    kill the player mercenary-brutality
  end when

  when the player's pole-escape expires, while the player is in the Top of the Post
    move the wandering mercenaries to the Base of the Center Post
    phrase pole-safe
    start the player's pole-problem
  end when

  when the player's pole-problem expires
    start the player's pole-destruction
  end when

  when the player's pole-destruction expires
    kill the player pole-falls
  end when
  when the player's market-escape expires
    phrase escape-caught
    kill the player mercenary-brutality
  end when

  when the player moves, while the wandering mercenaries is not here
    phrase merc-dash-away when the wandering mercenaries is approaching
    move the wandering mercenaries offstage
    change the wandering mercenaries to oblivious
    reset search
    reset lunge
    reset recovery
  end when

  on the player attacking
    refuse when the wandering mercenaries is oblivious: merc-dont-provoke
    phrase merc-break-free when the wandering mercenaries is aggressive
    phrase merc-shove-off when the wandering mercenaries is approaching
    change the wandering mercenaries to approaching
    reset lunge
    reset capture
    start recovery
  end on

  on the player talking while the wandering mercenaries is not oblivious
    refuse merc-ignore-pleas
  end on

create the sword
  aka swords, blade, blades, steel

  Not some decorated dandy's blades, these. Their steel is dull and tarnished,
  honed to a wicked edge. Nothing less than three-foot-long, practical tools
  for stabbing people dead.

  on the player taking
    refuse sword-not-for-you
  end on

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE WARY STALLKEEPERS
##
## The talk-refusals in the mercenaries' presence (`story.ni:1875-1879`): a
## keeper will not open the ST tree while the pair is standing in the room.
## Oblivious, chatting might draw their notice; once they've seen Jack, there
## is no time. Composed on the ten keepers in `grubbers-market.chord`. The
## mercenaries are offstage during the calm walk, so the tree plays freely
## there.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## The third refusal is the fruit stall's, not the sweep's (`story.ni:2434`:
## after the banana theft the keeper is too busy with his spilled fruit to
## talk). It lives here because the platform consults ONE interceptor per
## (entity, action) — `WorldModel.getInterceptorForAction` returns the
## highest-priority candidate only (GH #332) — so a second trait's `on
## talking`, or the keeper's own, never fires beside this one. The `when`
## names the carrier's situation; only the fruit stallkeeper ever stands in
## the Fruit Stall.

define trait wary
  on the player talking
    refuse when the wandering mercenaries is here and the wandering mercenaries is oblivious: merc-hush
    refuse when the wandering mercenaries is here: merc-run
    refuse when it is in the Fruit Stall and the Fruit Stall is chaotic: fruit-keeper-busy
    refuse when the player's market-escape has started: escape-no-time
  end on
end trait

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE KICK VERB
##
## `story.ni:553-563`: kicking is its own action that hands off to attacking,
## which is what lets `story.ni:2184` force the shin line when the verb is
## kick. Chord has no delegation, so the hand-off inverts: the `kick-escape`
## trait below mirrors the attack clause with the kick phrases, and kicking
## anything else falls to the platform's own can't-do-that line rather than
## the source's attack response — the one divergence, noted here.
##
## No `must be reachable` line, deliberately: that constraint's scope drops
## the player, and `kick me` (`story.ni:559`) has to parse. Kicking something
## out of reach falls to the same can't-do-that line.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define action kicking
  grammar
    kick the target

## The kick-specific break-free (`story.ni:2184-2185`): the source forces the
## shin line when the verb is kick, where a plain attack rolls the
## narrow-escape pool. Same mutations and postures as `on attacking it` above;
## a dispatch action's reactions live in traits, so this one is composed on
## the mercenaries.

define trait kick-escape
  on the player kicking
    refuse when it is oblivious: merc-dont-provoke
    phrase merc-break-free-kick when it is aggressive
    phrase merc-shove-off-kick when it is approaching
    change it to approaching
    reset the wandering mercenaries' lunge
    reset the wandering mercenaries' capture
    start the wandering mercenaries' recovery
  end on
end trait

## `kick me` (`story.ni:559`, the kicking yourself rule), composed on the
## player in `secret-letter.story`.

define trait kick-yourself
  on the player kicking
    refuse kick-self
  end on
end trait

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE CHASE — from TE20 to the slide (`story.ni:4003-4128`)
##
## The story moves `hunted` → `chase` on the TE20 trade (change document,
## "What moves hunted → chase": TE20, as in the source; the tent and pole
## timers are `chase` content). The sweep stops reading her the moment the
## chase begins — its arrival clock restarts only while `hunted` — and the
## source's rails take over, each a timer on the player:
##
##   * `tent-escape` — two turns to be out the back of the tent (one named
##     turn, then expiry). Still in the tent: caught, killed. At the Base:
##     the pole clock starts. Already at the Top: straight to the pole.
##   * `pole-escape` — one turn to be at the Top of the Post. Not there:
##     caught, killed. There: the pair takes the base and cannot climb.
##   * `pole-problem` — seven turns of texture below (six named turns and
##     the expiry), the source's `TIMER_Pole_Problem`.
##   * `pole-destruction` — the axe: two named turns spoken as they are
##     reached (POLE1, POLE2), then the expiry is POLE3 — the fall.
##
## Leaving the top by the cable resets the last two (`aerial-runway.chord`).
## The clocks' clauses are in the pair's block above; every line below is
## Gentry's except the three exit refusals, which the source enforced
## silently ("stop the action") and which are David's to write.
##
## Not carried: the axe as an examinable thing and the "You can't do
## anything to the mercenaries while you're way up here" refusals — an
## NPC-carried thing is out of the player's scope (GH #313), and the pair is
## a room below, out of scope anyway.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define timer tent-escape for the player
  fleeing
end timer

define timer pole-escape for the player
end timer

define timer pole-problem for the player
  muttering
  kicking
  shouting
  crowding
  debating
  running
  meanwhile
    phrase pole-texture
end timer

define timer pole-destruction for the player
  axe
    A mercenary enters the square behind Teisha's tent, carrying an axe.

    Uh-oh.
  chopping
    There is a solid chock! from below as the axe bites into wood, and the
    entire post shivers.
end timer

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## MARKET ESCAPE — after a seen landing (`story.ni:4132-4200`)
##
## The source's scene after the slide in Final Chase: a six-row spool of
## the mercenaries closing in, one row a turn, capture on the sixth; talking
## and stealing refused ("There's no time for that!"); the tent barred (a
## dash for it is turned back and she is thrown into a random adjacent
## stall); the alley and the north gates refused with the scene's own
## lines; a sprint line on every move; the scene ends safely at Commerce
## Street. In the port it opens on the three seen landings in `chase`
## (`aerial-runway.chord`, which reports the gap: with no place to change,
## the remake's spool can only end in capture). Row one prints on the
## landing turn itself, as the source's every-turn rule did; rows two to
## five are the timer's named turns; the expiry is row six and the death.
## Not carried: the stealing refusal (nothing is left to steal — the banana
## is already in the satchel before the trade); the source's `try looking`
## after the sprint line (the room prints itself). The tent: the source
## excluded only the tent from the random throw; here the tent's north is
## blocked while the spool runs, which both keeps the throw out of it
## (ADR-326 skips blocked exits) and answers a direct `n` with the same
## line. Every line is Gentry's.
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define timer market-escape for the player
  closer
    The shouts are getting closer.
  shoving
    You hear curses and yells of pain as the mercenaries shove their way
    through the crowds of shoppers. They're closing in!
  spotted
    Someone yells, 'There! I see him!'
  footsteps
    You hear thudding footsteps right behind you!
end timer

define phrase escape-shouting
  You hear shouting coming from all quarters of the market. If the
  mercenaries saw where you landed, they'll be after you in no time.
end phrase

define phrase escape-caught
  Mercenaries burst through the crowd from three different directions. They
  grab you and slam you to the ground, knocking the breath from your lungs.

  'Gotcha, you little runt!' someone growls in your ear.
end phrase

define phrase escape-no-time
  There's no time for that! You've got to run!
end phrase

define phrase escape-sprint, randomly
  You sprint through the crowd.
or
  You dive through the crowd.
or
  You weave through the crowd.
or
  You dodge through the crowd.
or
  You race through the crowd.
end phrase

define phrase escape-tent-planted
  You dash for Teisha's tent, but it's no good — the mercenaries have
  planted themselves outside it and are scanning the crowd. You backpedal
  quickly before they can spot you.
end phrase

define phrase escape-alley
  Not that way — the alley is a dead end!
end phrase

define phrase escape-north
  No good — the mercenaries have closed the north gates and aren't letting
  anyone through. Your only chance is east, further into the city!
end phrase

define phrase tent-caught
  'Jack, what are you doing?!' Teisha cries, 'Get out of—oof!'

  The mercenaries charge in, knocking Teisha and racks of silk garments to
  the floor. A rock-hard fist closes around your upper arm. 'Gotcha!' the
  mercenary yells. The other one starts hollering for his companions as
  they drag you out of the tent.
end phrase

define phrase mercenary-brutality
  The captain soon arrives, followed by two more of his men. They take your
  other arm as their leader smiles. 'Good work, dims. Got ourselves a nice
  little commission with this one.' And before you can react, he takes a
  sack of black cloth from his belt and jerks it down over your head.

  Everything goes dark. You can't breathe. You struggle, but you can no more
  break the iron grip on your arms than you could uproot a tree. The men
  holding you curse. Dimly, you hear shouts from the crowd — someone
  protesting this rough treatment of a child.

  'Don't worry, m'lady,' laughs the mercenary leader. 'We'll treat the
  little rat nice an' gentle. Like this, see?'

  Something huge and heavy crashes into your head, and the rest of your
  senses are ripped away. Why they wanted you, you never awake to find out.
end phrase

define phrase pole-escape-begins
  You can hear Teisha yelling shrilly at the mercenaries behind you — then
  her tirade is cut off abruptly amidst grunted curses. Someone is fumbling
  with the back flap of the tent!
end phrase

define phrase pole-caught
  The mercenaries burst out of the tent. With nowhere to run, you are
  quickly overwhelmed. They drag you back out to the market (dimly you see
  Teisha sitting amidst her scattered silks, nursing a bloody nose) and
  hold you firmly while calling to their comrades.
end phrase

define phrase pole-safe
  One of the mercenaries tries to climb after you, but can't get more than
  a few feet up before sliding back. Looks like you're safe for the time
  being — but they're already heatedly discussing how to get you down, and
  there are only so many ways you can go from here.
end phrase

define phrase pole-texture, randomly
  Another of the mercenaries tries his luck climbing the post, but his
  callused fingers can't get a grip on the wood.
or
  One of the mercenaries kicks the post in frustration.
or
  The mercenaries continue to shout and point.
or
  More mercenaries are squeezing into the tiny square behind Teisha's tent.
or
  The sounds of heated debate drift up from below. You just make out the
  word, 'fire'.
or
  The sounds of heated debate drift up from below. You just make out the
  word, 'ladder'.
or
  The sounds of heated debate drift up from below. You just make out the
  word, 'arrows'.
or
  One of the mercenaries runs back out through Teisha's tent.
end phrase

define phrase pole-falls
  The mercenaries chop ever faster, taking turns as each one grows tired.
  Soon the post is shaking and swaying dangerously, and it is all you can do
  to keep your balan-

  And that's when the post gives one horrendous lurch, your foot slips, and
  you go pinwheeling off the top of the post. You just have time to notice
  the grins on the mercenaries' faces, and the fact that none of them are
  trying to catch you, before the ground fills your sight and everything
  goes black.
end phrase

## The three ways back the source refused without a word during the chase
## (`story.ni:4030`, `:4044`): the tent's south, the base's south, the top's
## down. David's lines.

define phrase tent-no-way-back
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. Not back into the market — out the back way.)
end phrase

define phrase base-no-way-back
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. Not back into the tent; up.)
end phrase

define phrase top-no-way-down
  (PLACEHOLDER — David's line. The mercenaries are at the base of the post.)
end phrase

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE LINES — all Gentry's
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define phrase merc-arrival
  You notice a couple of the mercenaries pushing through the crowd, heading
  your way. They haven't seen you yet, but they're getting close.
end phrase

define phrase merc-spotted
  Suddenly one of the mercenaries does a double-take and sees you. 'There he
  is!' he yells, pointing, and both of them start shoving through the crowd
  towards you.
end phrase

define phrase merc-grab
  A rock-hard fist closes around your upper arm. 'Gotcha!' the mercenary
  yells. The other one starts hollering for his companions.
end phrase

## The struggle in the mercenary's grip (`story.ni:2168`): three random
## arms, ported verbatim — "arms skin" as Gentry wrote it.

define phrase merc-held, randomly
  You pull and struggle, but the man is just too strong.
or
  You beat on the mercenary's arms with your fists, but the man is just too strong.
or
  You claw the mercenary's arms skin with your fingernails, but the man is just too strong.
end phrase

define phrase merc-dash-away
  You dash away into the crowd, weaving between customers and pushing your way
  through the thickest knots of people. The mercenaries pound after you,
  yelling and shoving people out of their way, but they're too big and too
  slow, and you quickly manage to lose them.
end phrase

## The narrow escapes (`story.ni:2165-2168`). The source picks one at random
## and prefixes "Thinking quickly, you"; its first arm is the shin kick, which
## `kick` itself forces via the -kick phrases below.

define phrase merc-break-free, randomly
  Thinking quickly, you kick the mercenary in the shin.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' the mercenary's grip on your arm loosens,
  and you twist away. You're free!
or
  Thinking quickly, you elbow the mercenary as hard as you can, aiming below
  the belt.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' the mercenary's grip on your arm loosens,
  and you twist away. You're free!
or
  Thinking quickly, you spit at the mercenary's face, and a gob of spittle
  lands right in his eye.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' the mercenary's grip on your arm loosens,
  and you twist away. You're free!
or
  Thinking quickly, you scream your head off. People from two stalls away stop
  what they're doing to see what the commotion is, and when the mercenary
  clamps his hand over your mouth, you bite down hard.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' the mercenary's grip on your arm loosens,
  and you twist away. You're free!
end phrase

define phrase merc-shove-off, randomly
  Thinking quickly, you kick the mercenary in the shin.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' He stumbles backwards into his companion.
  Now they're both distracted; now's your chance to run!
or
  Thinking quickly, you elbow the mercenary as hard as you can, aiming below
  the belt.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' He stumbles backwards into his companion.
  Now they're both distracted; now's your chance to run!
or
  Thinking quickly, you spit at the mercenary's face, and a gob of spittle
  lands right in his eye.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' He stumbles backwards into his companion.
  Now they're both distracted; now's your chance to run!
or
  Thinking quickly, you scream your head off. People from two stalls away stop
  what they're doing to see what the commotion is, and when the mercenary
  clamps his hand over your mouth, you bite down hard.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' He stumbles backwards into his companion.
  Now they're both distracted; now's your chance to run!
end phrase

## The forced shin line (`story.ni:2184-2185`): deterministic where the pools
## above roll, one phrase per posture for the two tails.

define phrase merc-break-free-kick
  Thinking quickly, you kick the mercenary in the shin.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' the mercenary's grip on your arm loosens,
  and you twist away. You're free!
end phrase

define phrase merc-shove-off-kick
  Thinking quickly, you kick the mercenary in the shin.

  'Argh! Oh, you miserable runt—' He stumbles backwards into his companion.
  Now they're both distracted; now's your chance to run!
end phrase

define phrase merc-dont-provoke
  You resist the foolish impulse, reminding yourself that what you want is for
  them to *not* catch you.
end phrase

define phrase merc-ignore-pleas
  The mercenaries both ignore your pleas.
end phrase

define phrase merc-hush
  Better find somewhere else to chat; if you start making conversation, the
  mercenaries might take notice.
end phrase

define phrase merc-run
  No time to chat now; you've got to run!
end phrase

define phrase sword-not-for-you
  Even if you did manage to nick one of those swords without getting yourself
  totally killed, you wouldn't know what to do with it once you had it.
end phrase

## The seven idle lines (`story.ni:2077`). The seventh has its own inner
## random ("the other laughs bawdily / scowls / nods / snarls something back");
## Chord randomises whole arms, so it is expanded to four.

define phrase merc-idle, randomly
